'''Darren Bailey''' (born March 17, 1966)<ref name="101stILGA2">{{cite web |title=Representative Darren Bailey (R) 109th District |url=http://ilga.gov/house/Rep.asp?GA=101&MemberID=2757 |access-date=November 3, 2019 |work=101st Illinois General Assembly |archive-date=October 16, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191016054332/http://ilga.gov/house/Rep.asp?GA=101&MemberID=2757 |url-status=dead }}</ref> is an American politician who was a [[Republican Party (United States)|Republican]] member of the [[Illinois Senate]] for the 55th District and previously a member of the [[Illinois House of Representatives]] for the 109th District in [[Southern Illinois]]. Widely described by news outlets as a [[Far-right politics|far-right]] politician,<ref name=":42">{{Cite news |date=2022-06-21 |title=Illinois governor's race shapes up to be a battle of the billionaires |work=Financial Times |url=https://www.ft.com/content/5b9a2c74-aa63-4579-b349-e6d2c72af18e |access-date=2022-07-04}}</ref><ref name=":52">{{Cite news |last=Epstein |first=Reid J. |date=2022-06-26 |title=MAGA Voters Send a $50 Million G.O.P. Plan Off the Rails in Illinois |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/06/26/us/politics/illinois-governor-bailey-irvin-pritzker.html |access-date=2022-07-04 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref name=":62">{{Cite web |date=2022-06-10 |title=It's happening in Illinois: a far-right Republican leads race to be GOP candidate for governor |url=https://chicago.suntimes.com/columnists/2022/6/10/23163186/hard-right-republican-support-no-longer-rarity-illinois-darren-bailey-richard-irvin-governor |access-date=2022-07-04 |website=Chicago Sun-Times |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":72">{{Cite news |last=Epstein |first=Reid J. |date=2022-06-29 |title=Darren Bailey, a far-right state senator, will be the Republican nominee for Illinois governor. |language=en-US |work=The New York Times |url=https://www.nytimes.com/2022/06/28/us/politics/darren-bailey-illinois-governor-republican.html |access-date=2022-07-04 |issn=0362-4331}}</ref><ref name=":82">{{Cite web |last=Kapos |first=Shia |title=Illinois GOP takes big right turn with primary victories |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2022/06/29/illinois-gop-takes-big-right-turn-with-primary-victories-00043128 |access-date=2022-07-04 |website=POLITICO |date=June 29, 2022 |language=en}}</ref><ref name=":92">{{Cite news |last=Ax |first=Joseph |date=2022-07-26 |title=In risky midterm strategy, Democrats boost far-right candidate in Michigan race |language=en |work=Reuters |url=https://www.reuters.com/world/us/risky-midterm-strategy-democrats-boost-far-right-candidate-michigan-race-2022-07-26/ |access-date=2022-07-27}}</ref> he was the Republican nominee for the [[2022 Illinois gubernatorial election]], losing to Democrat [[JB Pritzker]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Illinois Election: Gov. JB Pritzker declares victory in re-election bid against Darren Bailey |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/chicago/news/illinois-election-governor-jb-pritzker-wins-darren-bailey/ |website=[[CBS News]] |date=November 9, 2022 }}</ref> Bailey is once again the Republican nominee in the [[2026 Illinois gubernatorial election|2026 election]].

Bailey ran in the Republican primary for the [[Illinois's 12th congressional district|12th congressional district]] in the [[2024 United States House of Representatives elections in Illinois#District 12|2024 U.S. House elections]], losing to incumbent [[Mike Bost]].<ref name=":4" />

==Early life and education==
Bailey was born in [[Louisville, Illinois]],<ref>{{cite web | title=About | url=https://repbailey.com/about/ | work=RepBailey.com | access-date=May 6, 2020 | archive-date=April 24, 2020 | archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200424170841/https://repbailey.com/about/ | url-status=dead }}</ref> on March 17, 1966.<ref name="101stILGA2"/> He graduated from [[North Clay High School]] and earned an [[Associate of Science]] degree in agricultural production from [[Lake Land College]].<ref name="101stILGA2"/>

==Political career==
Bailey, of [[Xenia, Illinois]], was a member of the North Clay Board of Education.<ref name="101stILGA2"/><ref name="Effingham Daily News Primary Win"/><ref>{{cite news|last=Hug|first=T.J.|title=Bailey challenges Reis for 109th district seat|newspaper=Navigator Journal|date=March 16, 2018|access-date=November 19, 2018|url=http://www.navigatorjournal.com/news/article_5605073e-2960-11e8-b8b3-ffe96bd139f1.html}}</ref> 

In a rare victory for candidates supported by [[Dan Proft]]'s [[Liberty Principles PAC]],<ref>{{cite news|last=Miller|first=Rich|title=The Proft Scorecard|date=March 21, 2018|newspaper=[[Capitol Fax]]|access-date=November 19, 2018|url=https://capitolfax.com/2018/03/22/the-proft-scorecard/}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|last=Korecki|first=Natasha|title='I know he's frustrated': GOP megadonor on staggering losing streak|date=August 17, 2018|newspaper=[[Politico]]|access-date=November 19, 2018|url=https://www.politico.com/story/2018/08/17/richard-uihlein-gop-donor-losing-candidates-784146}}</ref> Bailey defeated [[David Reis]] in the 2018 Republican primary.<ref name="Effingham Daily News Primary Win">{{cite news|last=Stewart|first=Keith|title=Bailey defeats Reis in 109th District GOP primary|date=March 20, 2018|newspaper=[[Effingham Daily News]]|access-date=November 19, 2018|url=https://www.effinghamdailynews.com/news/2018_elections/bailey-defeats-reis-in-th-district-gop-primary/article_09da1fe8-2cc1-11e8-908c-7becd47359d1.html}}</ref> Bailey then defeated Democratic candidate Cynthia Given, the Secretary of the Richland County Democratic Party,<ref>{{cite news|last=Miller|first=Rich|title=Two candidates who deserve notice… and respect|date=October 31, 2018|newspaper=[[Capitol Fax]]|access-date=November 19, 2018|url=https://capitolfax.com/2018/10/31/two-candidates-who-deserve-notice-and-respect/}}</ref> by a margin of 76.14% to 23.86%.<ref name="2018gen">{{cite web | title=2018 General Election Results | url=https://www.elections.il.gov/ElectionOperations/ElectionVoteTotals.aspx?ID=JVM3BFfJbYc%3d&OfficeType=Mzn1hIdK%2bXutYfUM2d1xMYNZRN8UQ%2fvLRNQyffOVr6U%3d | work=Illinois State Board of Elections | access-date=December 2, 2019 }}{{Dead link|date=March 2023 |bot=InternetArchiveBot |fix-attempted=yes }}</ref> The 109th District at the time, located in the [[Wabash Valley|Illinois Wabash Valley]], included all of [[Edwards County, Illinois|Edwards]], [[Jasper County, Illinois|Jasper]], [[Richland County, Illinois|Richland]], [[Wabash County, Illinois|Wabash]], [[Wayne County, Illinois|Wayne]], and [[White County, Illinois|White]] counties and parts of [[Effingham County, Illinois|Effingham]] and [[Lawrence County, Illinois|Lawrence]] counties.<ref>{{cite web|url=http://elections.il.gov/Downloads/VotingInformation/PDF/2011Districts/2011RepDist109.pdf|title=Representative District 109|publisher=[[Illinois State Board of Elections]]|date=May 18, 2011|access-date=November 19, 2018|archive-date=February 12, 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170212031649/http://elections.il.gov/Downloads/VotingInformation/PDF/2011Districts/2011RepDist109.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref name="Announcement">{{cite news|author=<!--Staff writer(s); no by-line.-->|url=http://www.olneydailymail.com/news/20170913/darren-bailey-announces-run-for-state-representative-in-109th-district|title=Darren Bailey Announces Run for State Representative in the 109th District|work=Olney Daily Mail|access-date=November 19, 2018|archive-date=November 19, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181119214652/http://www.olneydailymail.com/news/20170913/darren-bailey-announces-run-for-state-representative-in-109th-district|url-status=dead}}</ref>

On July 8, 2019, Bailey announced his intention to run for the [[Illinois Senate]] seat being vacated by [[Dale Righter]].<ref>{{cite news|last=Miller|first=Rich|title=Minister who delivered controversial House prayer to run for House|date=July 8, 2019|newspaper=[[Capitol Fax]]|access-date=July 8, 2019|url=https://capitolfax.com/2019/07/08/minister-who-delivered-controversial-house-prayer-to-run-for-house/}}</ref> He won the March 17, 2020, Republican primary.<ref>{{cite web | last1=Perry | first1=Scott | title=Area wrap: Bailey earns 55th district nod, and other area results from Tuesday's primary election | url=https://herald-review.com/news/state-and-regional/govt-and-politics/area-wrap-bailey-earns-55th-district-nod-and-other-area-results-from-tuesdays-primary-election/article_86820db0-e3fc-57fd-af84-e90965f0d624.html | date=March 18, 2020 | work=[[Herald & Review]] | access-date=May 6, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | last1=Cordes | first1=Kaitlin | title=Bailey wins 55th District Republican nod | url=https://www.effinghamdailynews.com/news/2020_election/bailey-wins-th-district-republican-nod/article_d0982aae-68af-11ea-970f-7fbceb499cde.html | date=March 17, 2020 | work=[[Effingham Daily News]] | access-date=May 6, 2020}}</ref> Bailey defeated Democratic nominee Cynthia Given in the general election.<ref>{{cite web | last1=Griffith | first1=Cathy | title=Bailey wins 55th Senate seat | url=https://www.effinghamdailynews.com/news/local_news/bailey-wins-55th-senate-seat/article_5125cfc0-1e43-11eb-88e8-1f1be4facfee.html | date=November 3, 2020 | work=[[Effingham Daily News]] | access-date=November 7, 2020}}</ref>

In April 2020, Bailey sued Governor [[JB Pritzker]], claiming that the governor's stay-at-home order extension to mitigate effects of the [[COVID-19 pandemic in the United States|COVID-19 pandemic]] was unfairly affecting residents of [[Clay County, Illinois|Clay County]]. A judge granted a temporary restraining order against the stay-at-home order, though it only applied to Bailey.<ref>{{cite web | last1=Corley | first1=Cheryl | title=Illinois Lawmaker Files Lawsuit; Wants Stay-At-Home Rules Lifted | url=https://www.npr.org/sections/coronavirus-live-updates/2020/04/28/847620047/illinois-lawmaker-files-lawsuit-wants-stay-at-home-rules-lifted | date=April 28, 2020 | work=[[NPR]] | access-date=May 6, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | last1=Pearson | first1=Rick | last2=Munks | first2=Jamie | last3=Petrella | first3=Dan | title=Southern Illinois judge temporarily blocks Gov. J.B. Pritzker's stay-at-home order from applying to Republican state lawmaker who sued | url=https://www.chicagotribune.com/coronavirus/ct-coronavirus-illinois-stay-at-home-lawsuit-20200427-luf5hsnhpff7harnkckfopq26u-story.html | date=April 28, 2020 | work=[[Chicago Tribune]] | access-date=May 6, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | last1=Tareen | first1=Sophia | last2=O'Connor | first2=John | title=Judge rules Pritzker's stay-at-home order an overreach | url=https://apnews.com/761aded9e0bfec12057bb22fe65ff145 | date=April 27, 2020 | work=[[Associated Press]] | access-date=May 6, 2020}}</ref> Pritzker stated he would appeal the order and characterized Bailey's lawsuit as a "cheap political stunt".<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nbcchicago.com/news/coronavirus/pritzker-blasts-bailey-lawsuit-as-cheap-political-stunt-vows-swift-appeal/2263207/|title=Pritzker Blasts Bailey Lawsuit as 'Cheap Political Stunt,' Vows Swift Appeal|website=NBC Chicago|date=April 28, 2020 |language=en-US|access-date=April 29, 2020}}</ref> On May 20, 2020, the Illinois House voted 81–27 to remove Bailey from its session for refusing to wear a mask.<ref>{{cite web | last1=Klar | first1=Rebecca | title=Illinois House removed GOP rep from legislative session after he refused to wear a mask: 'A callous disregard for life' | url=https://thehill.com/homenews/state-watch/498889-illinois-house-removed-gop-rep-from-legislative-session-after-he-refused | date=May 20, 2020 | work=[[The Hill (newspaper)|The Hill]] | access-date=May 21, 2020}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | last1=Stimson | first1=Brie | title=Illinois House votes to remove Republican rep from session for refusing to wear mask | url=https://www.foxnews.com/politics/republican-state-representative-removed-from-legislative-session-for-refusing-to-wear-mask | date=May 21, 2020 | work=[[Fox News]] | access-date=May 21, 2020}}</ref> The following day, Bailey attended the House wearing a face mask as required by the rules.<ref>{{cite web | title=House lawmaker returns to session with mask | url=https://www.sj-r.com/news/20200521/house-lawmaker-returns-to-session-with-mask | date=May 21, 2020 | work=[[The State Journal-Register]] | access-date=May 22, 2020}}</ref>

On February 22, 2021, Bailey announced his candidacy for governor in [[2022 Illinois gubernatorial election|2022]].<ref name="GovernorRun">{{cite web | last1=Pearson | first1=Rick | title=Conservative Republican state Sen. Darren Bailey announces governor run, pledges to fight 'political elites' | url=https://www.chicagotribune.com/politics/ct-illinois-governor-race-bailey-20210223-4bx7cetz4zdqzffph5sjlsaury-story.html | date=February 22, 2021 | work=[[Chicago Tribune]] | access-date=February 23, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | last1=Sullender | first1=Andrew | title=Downstate Republican Bailey kicks off bid for governor, vowing to speak for the ignored: 'We've been used. We've been mocked.' | url=https://chicago.suntimes.com/politics/2021/2/22/22296413/downstate-republican-darren-bailey-governor-campaign-effingham-pritzker | date=February 22, 2021 | work=[[Chicago Sun-Times]] | access-date=February 23, 2021}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | title=Illinois Sen. Darren Bailey Announces Run for Governor | url=https://www.nbcchicago.com/news/local/illinois-sen-darren-bailey-announces-run-for-governor/2444680/ | date=February 22, 2021 | work=[[WMAQ-TV]] | access-date=February 23, 2021}}</ref> On December 13, 2021, Bailey announced that his running mate for lieutenant governor would be former WLS-AM 890 talk radio host [[Stephanie Trussell]].<ref>{{Cite web|last=Hinton|first=Rachel|date=2021-12-13|title=GOP gov candidate Bailey names former talk radio host as running mate|url=https://chicago.suntimes.com/politics/2021/12/13/22832463/gop-governor-candidate-darren-bailey-running-mate-stephanie-trussell-former-talk-radio-host|access-date=2021-12-17|website=Chicago Sun-Times|language=en}}</ref> Three days before the primary, Bailey appeared at a rally with Donald Trump and received his endorsement.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-06-26 |title=Trump endorses Darren Bailey for Illinois governor |url=https://www.wbez.org/stories/trump-endorses-darren-bailey-for-illinois-governor/7c4ea502-a611-4db5-abb0-65bd8c851f7a |access-date=2022-07-07 |website=WBEZ Chicago |language=en}}</ref>

Bailey won the primary by a large margin, receiving 57.7% of the vote and winning all but two counties.<ref name="mckinney">{{Cite news |last=McKinney |first=Dave |date=2022-06-28 |title=Conservative Illinois state Sen. Darren Bailey wins GOP primary for governor |language=en |work=NPR |url=https://www.npr.org/2022/06/27/1107063641/illinois-governor-primary-democrat-pritzker-gop-bailey-griffin-irvin-trump |access-date=2022-07-04}}</ref>

Before a campaign appearance on July 4, 2022, in the wake of the [[Highland Park parade shooting|Highland Park Parade Shooting]], Bailey stated on a livestream "The shooter is still at large, so let's pray for justice to prevail, and then let's move on and let's celebrate - celebrate the independence of this nation". He received backlash for his statement and later apologized.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-07-06 |title=Trump-backed Illinois candidate says 'let's move on' just hours after parade shooting |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/news/world/americas/us-politics/darren-bailey-highland-park-shooting-trump-b2115809.html |access-date=2022-07-07 |website=The Independent |language=en}}</ref> For his gubernatorial campaign, Bailey received more than $50M in support from businessman [[Dick Uihlein]].<ref>{{Cite web |title=Darren Bailey's campaign gets $2M boost from conservative megadonor Richard Uihlein |url=https://www.chicagotribune.com/politics/elections/ct-darren-bailey-richard-uihlein-contribution-20221020-rc6gt6mkdra5xfpveuvrvrxtja-story.html |access-date=2022-10-30 |website=Chicago Tribune |date=October 19, 2022 }}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-10-12 |title=GOP megadonor tops $50M in donations to Darren Bailey, conservative super-PAC |url=https://abc7chicago.com/jb-pritzker-darren-bailey-illinois-governor-richard-uihlein/12317194/ |access-date=2022-10-30 |website=ABC7 Chicago |language=en}}</ref>

Bailey ended up losing to incumbent J.B. Pritzker by a margin of 12.5 percentage points. Despite his loss, Bailey improved upon [[Bruce Rauner]]'s [[2018 Illinois gubernatorial election|2018 performance]] by around 4% and flipped four counties that Pritzker had won in 2018. Bailey also outperformed his polling aggregate by 3.7%.

In the state Senate, Bailey served on the following committees: Agriculture (Minority Spokesperson); Education; Energy and Public Utilities; Health; Higher Education; Labor; Subcommittee on Public Health; App- Agriculture, Envir. & Energy; App- Higher Education; App-Human Services; App- Personnel and Procurement; Redistricting- E Central & SE IL.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Illinois General Assembly - Senator Biography |url=https://www.ilga.gov/senate/Senator.asp?GA=102&MemberID=2989 |access-date=2021-12-01 |website=www.ilga.gov}}</ref>

In 2023, Bailey announced that he would run against incumbent U.S. Representative [[Mike Bost]] in the Republican primary for the [[Illinois's 12th congressional district|12th congressional district]] in [[2024 United States House of Representatives elections in Illinois|2024]].<ref name=":4">{{Cite web |last=Kapos |first=Shia |date=2023-07-04 |title=Illinois Republican Darren Bailey challenges Rep. Mike Bost |url=https://www.politico.com/news/2023/07/04/illinois-darren-bailey-mike-bost-00104658 |access-date=2023-07-04 |website=POLITICO |language=en}}</ref> On March 19, 2024, Bailey lost the primary to Bost.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://apnews.com/article/mike-bost-illinois-darren-bailey-congress-3a395e4e3afabad71f22bca8ec84e695|title=Congressman Mike Bost survives competitive GOP primary challenge to win nomination for sixth term|location=[[Springfield, Illinois]]|publisher=[[Associated Press]]|date=March 20, 2024|access-date=September 11, 2025}}</ref>

In September 2025, Bailey announced his intent to seek the Republican nomination in the [[2026 Illinois gubernatorial election]]. He ended up comfortably winning the primary in March 2026, with Cook County Chairman Aaron del Mar as his running mate.<ref name=bailey>{{cite news |last1=Ahern |first1=MaryAnn |title=Darren Bailey to seek Republican gubernatorial nomination |url=https://www.nbcchicago.com/news/local/chicago-politics/darren-bailey-to-seek-republican-gubernatorial-nomination-source/3821307/ |date=September 8, 2025 |access-date=October 23, 2025 |work=[[WMAQ-TV]] |language=en}}</ref>

== Political positions ==
Bailey holds [[Far-right politics|far-right]] political views.<ref name=":42" /><ref name=":52" /><ref name=":62" /><ref name=":72" /><ref name=":82" /><ref name=":92" /> He [[United States anti-abortion movement|opposes abortion]], and as a state lawmaker, he has voted against abortion rights measures.<ref name=":3">{{Cite web |last=Ma |first=Jessica |date=2022-06-28 |title=Illinois Gubernatorial Primaries Candidates views on Abortion |url=https://dailynorthwestern.com/2022/06/28/city/the-daily-northwesterns-guide-to-illinois-2022-governors-race-candidates-on-abortion/ |access-date=2022-07-20 |website=[[The Daily Northwestern]]}}</ref><ref name=":2">{{Cite web |last=Kozlov |first=Dana |date=27 June 2022 |title=After Roe v. Wade was overturned, where do the candidates for Illinois governor stand on abortion? |url=https://www.cbsnews.com/chicago/news/after-roe-v-wade-was-overturned-where-do-the-candidates-for-illinois-governor-stand-on-abortion/ |access-date=2022-07-20 |website=[[CBS News]] |language=en-US}}</ref> He praised the Supreme Court's 2022 decision to [[Dobbs v. Jackson Women's Health Organization|overturn ''Roe v. Wade'']].<ref>{{Cite web |date=2022-06-24 |title=Republican candidates for governor say 'historic' overturn of Roe v. Wade signals 'battle for life ... moves right here to Illinois' |url=https://chicago.suntimes.com/elections/2022/6/24/23182313/republican-governor-abortion-roe-wade-bailey-sullivan-irvin-rabine-schimpf-solomon-life-choice |access-date=2022-07-20 |website=[[Chicago Sun-Times]] |language=en}}</ref> He supports a statewide ban on abortion except in cases where the mother's life is in danger, opposing abortion in cases of rape or incest.<ref name=":2" /><ref>{{Cite web |title=Election 2022: Abortion Is Central in 1st Post-Roe Primaries |url=https://news.wttw.com/2022/06/28/election-2022-abortion-central-1st-post-roe-primaries |access-date=2022-07-20 |website=The Associated Press |language=en |via=[[WTTW News]]}}</ref> In a video posted on Facebook in 2017, Bailey said "the attempted [[Holocaust|extermination of the Jews]] of [[World War II]] doesn't even compare on a shadow of the life that has been lost with abortion since its legalization".<ref>{{Cite web |title=Holocaust 'doesn't even compare' to abortion deaths, says GOP nominee for Illinois governor |url= https://forward.com/fast-forward/512727/holocaust-doesnt-even-compare-to-abortion-deaths-says-gop-nominee-for-illinois-governor/ |access-date=2022-08-02|website=Forward|date= August 2022 }}</ref>

Bailey strongly opposes the [[SAFE-T Act]], a bill signed into law by Governor [[J. B. Pritzker]] that, among other things, ended mandatory cash [[bail]] in Illinois.<ref>{{cite web| url = https://www.hoiabc.com/2022/09/07/bailey-vows-repeal-safe-t-act/| title = Bailey vows to repeal Safe-T Act| last = Lopez| first = Tyler| date = September 6, 2022| website = HOIABC| publisher = ABC| access-date = September 12, 2022}}</ref> Bailey has referred to the act as "radical, pro-criminal legislation".<ref>{{cite web| url = https://prairiestatewire.com/stories/631374361-bailey-the-safe-t-act-is-continually-being-fought-against-as-a-radical-pro-criminal-legislation| title = Bailey: 'The SAFE-T Act is continually being fought against as a radical, pro-criminal legislation'| last = Gonzalez| first = Bree| date = September 7, 2022| publisher = Prairie State Wire| access-date = September 12, 2022}}</ref>

In 2019, Bailey and seven other Republicans sponsored a resolution calling for the City of Chicago to become its own state, claiming that "the majority of residents in [[downstate Illinois]] disagree with City of Chicago residents on key issues such as gun ownership, abortion, immigration, and other policy issues."<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |last=Sullender |first=Andrew |date=2021-05-25 |title=Republican Bailey's message to Chicago evolves from get lost to make Chicago great |url=https://chicago.suntimes.com/elections/2021/5/25/22453983/republican-darren-bailey-chicago-51st-state-secede-illinois-resolution-governor |access-date=2021-07-21 |website=Chicago Sun-Times |language=en}}</ref> This was not the first time such a resolution was introduced, but it had little chance of passing, and some of the sponsors stated they did not actually intend to separate Chicago.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Lloyd |first=Whitney |title=Resolution proposes separating Chicago from Illinois to create 51st state |url=https://abcnews.go.com/US/resolution-proposes-separating-chicago-illinois-create-51st-state/story?id=62405752 |access-date=2022-08-31 |website=ABC News |language=en}}</ref> With the announcement of Bailey's bid for governor, Bailey backtracked, calling it "an old resolution" and "a warning shot" targeted towards Chicago.<ref name=":0" />

Following the [[2020 United States presidential election|2020 presidential election]], Bailey refused to acknowledge that [[Joe Biden]] fairly defeated [[Donald Trump]], stating that he "did not know" if Trump's disproven [[Attempts to overturn the 2020 United States presidential election|claims of voter fraud]] were legitimate.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Pearson |first=Rick |date=19 August 2021 |title=Illinois Republicans at state fair take optimistic route heading into next year's 'do-or-die election' |url=https://pantagraph.com/news/state-and-regional/govt-and-politics/illinois-republicans-at-state-fair-take-optimistic-route-heading-into-next-year-s-do-or/article_2f416a1f-c843-553e-a294-870fa2eb8ee6.html |url-status=live |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210824144540/https://www.pantagraph.com/news/state-and-regional/govt-and-politics/illinois-republicans-at-state-fair-take-optimistic-route-heading-into-next-year-s-do-or/article_2f416a1f-c843-553e-a294-870fa2eb8ee6.html |archive-date=August 24, 2021 |access-date=2021-09-26 |website=Chicago Tribune |language=en |via=[[The Pantagraph]]}}</ref>

